1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,080
Hi everyone, I'd like to show you a nice new feature that we've added to Burp Suite.

2
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:18,000
This is to pretty print JSON and other content in the HTTP message editor.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,320
I'm going to the PortsWigger website and clicking on Daily Swig.

4
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:32,320
Daily Swig is our new site covering cybersecurity, breaches, bug bounties and other security news.

5
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:39,320
If we look under the hood, the feed of stories on the front page is actually being loaded using JSON.

6
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:45,320
Here we can see in Burp Suite what the JSON coming from the website actually looks like.

7
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:49,320
And it's pretty hard to read. Everything's flattened onto one line.

8
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:54,320
You can't see the structure of the data. It's hard to see the keys and values.

9
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:59,320
With the new Burp feature, the JSON now looks like this.

10
00:00:59,320 --> 00:01:03,320
It's nicely formatted and much more readable.

11
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:07,320
You can easily see the structure and read the contents.

12
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:17,320
The same applies to other data formats like JavaScript, CSS, HTML, XML.

13
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:24,320
It also works for HTTP requests containing data in JSON or other formats.

14
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:30,320
You can toggle between pretty printing and the original view by clicking the pretty button.

15
00:01:30,320 --> 00:01:36,320
We'll be adding a bunch more prettifying features like this in future.

16
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:40,320
As always, do let us know if you have any feedback. And have fun!

